// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  Voltade — canonical database schema (source of truth)
// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//
//  WHY PRISMA SCHEMA IS THE SOURCE OF TRUTH
//  ----------------------------------------
//  Every table/column/index below is what production runs. `prisma/migrations/*/
//  migration.sql` is the DDL that materialises it on PostgreSQL 16, and a test
//  (packages/db/test/schema-parity.test.ts) parses THIS file and compares it,
//  column by column, against `information_schema` of a live database that has
//  just run those migrations. So the three artefacts — schema, SQL, live DB —
//  cannot drift without a red build. That gate exists because the sandbox this
//  repo was written in cannot reach binaries.prisma.sh, so `prisma migrate diff`
//  could not be used to generate the SQL; a machine check replaces it.
//
//  CONVENTIONS
//  -----------
//  · Physical names are snake_case (`@@map` / `@map`) because that is what the
//    SQL driver, the DBA and `psql` all read. Prisma's camelCase stays in code.
//  · Public entities use `String @id @default(cuid())`: non-enumerable ids mean
//    a scraper cannot walk `/api/games/1..N` and a leaked id leaks nothing about
//    table size. Append-only, high-volume tables (plays, stats, logs, xp) use
//    `BigInt @id @default(autoincrement())` — 8 bytes beats 25, and nothing
//    outside the server ever sees them.
//  · Counters (plays, likes_count, rating_avg, games_count…) are denormalised
//    onto the parent row and updated with `SET x = x + 1` in the same
//    transaction. Sorting 20k+ games by "most played" must be an index scan,
//    never a `COUNT(*) … GROUP BY` per request.
//  · `deleted_at` = soft delete on rows a moderator may need to restore
//    (users, games, comments, posts, pages). Every read path filters it.
//  · Postgres enums (not check-constraints, not strings) so an invalid status is
//    physically unwritable.
// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

generator client {
  provider = "prisma-client-js"
}

datasource db {
  provider = "postgresql"
  url      = env("DATABASE_URL")
}

// ───────────────────────────────── enums ─────────────────────────────────

